From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Mio <mio@mio19.uk>
Date: Sat, 24 Oct 2026 21:12:47 +1100
Subject: [PATCH] harden the app bridge D-Bus policy and frame validation
 (overlay/asteroidos)

- org.asteroidos.btsyncd.conf: drop the allow-all default policy. Only
  root and the unprivileged ceres session user, which runs
  asteroid-btsyncd as a systemd user service and the watch apps, may own,
  send to or receive from org.asteroidos.btsyncd.
- appbridgeservice: validate every received frame (total size cap of 512
  bytes, magic, appId/actionLen bounds, token charset, payload cap),
  allow only the camera and spo2 app ids, and for camera accept only
  ready, paused, photo_captured, video_started and video_stopped from
  the phone. sendMessage/sendBytes reject unknown app ids and, for
  camera, any action outside capture, flip, zoom_in, zoom_out, torch,
  mode_photo, mode_video and status_query. Camera frames never carry a
  payload. Payload contents are never logged.
- camera-shutter.sh: accept only the allowlisted actions (default
  capture) and call busctl directly, without gdbus/dbus-send fallbacks.
- app-bridge.patch main.cpp: keep registering org.asteroidos.btsyncd on
  the system bus, since the helper tools and the camera app address the
  daemon by that name, but restore the hard error (exit(1)) on failure
  instead of continuing with a warning. The warning only existed
  because the old policy let nobody except root own the name; with
  ceres allowed to own it, failing to register means the bridge is
  unusable and must not go unnoticed. Dropping the change entirely is
  not an option as upstream has the registration commented out.
